swarren@convex.COM writes:
  >[]If a genie asked you what you wished for and you replied, "a tinning-kit",
  >would he notice the hyphen in your statement and refuse your wish?
  >Last time I checked hyphens don't appear suspended in the air in front
  >of my mouth when I converse.
  >When I asked my wife for my grey suit she wasn't confused by the "e"
  >in grey.  She just handed me the gray one...[]

Although I agree that spelling should not prevent someone from obtaining an
item, I think that the listing of all items available for wishing would be 
"giving too much away".  Part of the fun of playing hack is occasionally 
finding a new and exciting weapon or wand to play around with.  
Opinions may differ on this....

Anyway, my suggestion goes something like this:
  As Steve points out above, people don't care what spelling of a word you use
when you talk, just so long as they understand what you mean.  Why not have
hack use "sound-alike" routines to interpret what the user wants and turn it
into something the game understands?  Then the game could check its 
interpretation with the user.  This approach would not "give too much away",
but would allow users to get the stuff they want without knowing _exactly_ how
to spell it.  They would have to be _close_, though.
  I know that sound-alike routines exist, but I imagine that it would take
quite a bit of work to implement this.  (Is this an understatement?)  It would
probably be preferable to make hack-specific ones.

kevin@cfctech.cfc.com writes:
  >[]I did want to point out something, though - NetHack would IMHO be a total
  >travesty if, for the sake of wishing, it had to include a full-blown Natural
  >Language Parser (NLP). Just ask some of the dudes over in comp.ai.* how much
  >code that would take in C.[]

Agreed.  I think that some range of sound-alike processing could be made
specific enough to NetHack that it wouldn't be _enormous_.  However, I am
*not* one of the dudes from com.ai.*, so I can't say that I know what
I'm talking about.

Example:
Bob Hacker is finds a wand 'o wishing and zaps it.
  You may wish for something.  Whatcha want?
(Bob)    grey dragon scale mail
           ^
  Sorry, pal.  Nothing like that in this game.  --More--
(Hack recognizes that these words sound alike...)
  Would gray dragon scale mail interest you? [yn]    << user could be given a
	  ^                                           | listing if more than
(Bob)   y                                             | one item "sounded" like
  you receive: Gray dragon scale mail.                | his request.

(This would obviously get more complex with enchantments and blessed/cursed 
items)

Would this be a good approach for genocide as well?  Comments?  I don't even
know if this is a workable idea, I just wanted to offer it up as a suggestion.
(In other words, don't flame me if this is silly)
